CountDownLatch

- A bit like semaphore but specialized, it accepts, a counter
- CountDownLatch countDownLatch = new CountDownLatch(counter)
- Provides functionality of decreasing the counter
- countDownLatch.countDown()
- countDownLatch.await() - blocks currently executing thread, when count down reaches 0, await will be unblocked
- mostly used for subproblems executed in parallel, when a sub problem is solved we call countDown marking the fact that 
that one was solved
- We don't have any way of increasing the counter once it reaches 0
- One shot tool

In search a num in array problem, we divide the portions of array to worker threads to search in.
Once the thread completes the work, we call countDown to denote that this thread has completed searching
At the end in main thread, we wait for all threads to complete before printing the search posiion by using await()
Once all threads complete their work, the count down will become 0 and await() will unblock
At that point we can print results.

The idea behind this concept is, when each sub problem is solved, we count down to indicate that the sub problem was solved.
One shot problems that can split into multiple problems that can run in parallel.


Barriers

- CyclicBarrier cyclicBarrier = new CyclicBarrier(num)
- As a barrier, it will block the threads so in constructor we provide number of threads
- cyclicBarrier.await(), after all threads hit the barrier, they will be released by the barrier
and continue the execution
- Idea is simple all threads will reach barrier before they are released for execution. 
- Importantly it can be reused, as the name suggests, it is cyclic in nature and will reset on its own.
- CyclicBarrier cyclicBarrier = new CyclicBarrier(num, runnable) runnable will be called when barrier is released

We solved a problem where we had a 2d matrix, for each cell, we added all elements of previous row + current cell.
Each thread was responsible for updating one column. After updating one cell in that column, we would wait for other threads to update their cells.
Once all threads update, barrier will release and thread will move on to updating the next cell in the column.
Reason why we would need to wait for each thread to complete is because we want to do previous row sum + current cell 

Essentially,
Wait until 4 threads call barrier.await().
Once the 4th thread arrives, release all 4 threads.
Then reset the barrier so it can be used again.


Phasers

- Phaser phaser = new Phaser()
- It is a bit like barrier but more flexible. When we create CyclicBarrier, we need to provide the barrier count. 
- In phaser, it does not require number of barrier threads in its constructor.
- phaser.register() increments barrier number by 1. Thread can register itself and express that they want to be part of barrier.
- phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance() - blocks untill all registered threads arrive at this method, equivalent of await() in cyclic barrier
- Each time barrier is released, it increments internal count called phase. phaser.getPhase() gives count. 
- phaser.waitAdvance(), phaser.bulkRegister(), we can also construct trees of phasers - phaser chaining.
- phaser.arriveAndDeregister() - thread will deregister from phaser after arriving, num of barrier count will decrease by 1. 
Thread can decide to not be part of barrier anymore. Non-blocking

Map-reduce like problem where we will have an array where we double each numb (*2) and then sum the entire array
We will have phase 1 which will double each element, each thread will handle one element
Phase 2 will have sum by single thread with index 0


Exchangers

- Exchaner<String> exchanger = new Exchanger<>();
- exchange data between 2 threads
- method String recvd1 = exchanger.exchange("value1"), String recvd2 = exchanger.exchange("value2")
- thread1 will call exchange and block until other thread reaches the exchange method and then both will exchange value
- threads act like they reach barrier, but they will also exchange data
- at any given time, only 2 threads can exchange data
- we need to make sure that we always have pairs of threads that can reach this method otherwise there will be some threads stuck in waiting


Deadlock
- situation where threads are blocked. 
- synchronization primitives in non consistent way can also cause this problem
- Thread.currentThread.join() - grab current thread and wait for it to finish, recommended to use join(timeout)


Volatile
- 2 threads executing on different cpu can access a var on mainmemory, it may take 100 ns for cpu to grab that data from main memory.
to reduce this tme, there's cache present on cpu chip so var is copied on cpu chip from mainmemory. This access time is 7ns
- now, shared var on mainmemory and both threads are trying to read that, and there are multiple read oprs, cpu will optimize by storing that
var on cache to reduce access time. If one thread writes to it, visibility of that write for specific time will be only on cache, it won't be on cpu
- propogation to main mem wont happen immediately, now if other thread is trying to read that ar, it will see stale data
- volatile keyword, the read will happen from main memory, write will be stored on main memory. It won't be copied over to cache.
- to gain consistency, we will lose on performance
















